Here's an update on release issues:

Releasing P?S/8 is fairly difficult without a lot of personal attention.  
The logistics are quite extensive, especially if you really don't have a 
pre-exisiting medium or mechanism.

My original intention was that the P?S Enhanced PDP-8 Simulator (PEPS) for 
Windows (XP through 10) would be completely released and within it 
currently is P?S/8 Version 8Z (with recent patches) along with OS/8 V3D 
(3Q).  [For more information, you should already be in the pepswin grouip 
or ask for an add directly here and now, etc.  That will give some of the 
details in the way.

Without going into great detail, that package is nearly completed, and 
completely useful to within nitpicks.  That said, here is what the 
"nitpicks" are:

1) The package works 100% as-is on Windows XP and Windows Vista.  For 
Windows 7, 8 and 10 you need to apply a registry patch and procedure that 
to date I do by hand.  It works completely fine on Windows 7.

That said, it needs testing on Windows 8 and then separately on Windows 
10.  there is a possibility that what has to be done on the other two 
systems will somewhat differ from what is done on Windows 7.  Time will 
tell.  Some of this work was performed as recently as this last September.

Once the two additional systems are tested and confirmed working (which may 
require independent documentation of anything specifically is different 
from Windows 7) this all has to be documented for all five systems 
separately (the documentation requires at the least a figure in the Guide 
document for each of them regarding the recommended starting configuration] 
Then I also have to finish writing the underlying BATCH file that makes it 
all happen.

At this point, that part is written to the point of knowing that your 
system is XP or it is Vista and able to issue a message that you don't need 
the actual registry changes.  I have to (at least) translate the manual 
procedure into BATCH-oriented sub-commands including translating registry 
patches done manually and/or with REGEDIT into the somewhat more obscure 
[and operationally quite different, albeit automatic] REG program that 
takes iinline command-line arguments.

At this point it could be complicated by having as many as three sets of 
these changes assuming 7 is different from 8 is different from 10, etc. [Or 
to be optimistic, proving it is not XP or Vista is sufficient, but this has 
to be tested].

But once all these details are worked out, there is already a placeholder 
for setting up for removal of automatic auto-arrange coupled with align to 
grid.  Align to grid is modestly useful, but the point is to be able to 
turn off auto-arrange; for myself i prefer the align to grid, but this is 
personal preference; the main issue is that the feature to prevent the 
auto-arrange ]when the view involves icons of any size; the entire issue is 
moot if you prefer list or details or even tiles view, but it is arguably 
much harder to use these by nature on the main control 
window/command-center] thus, the recommendation is start with the "serving 
suggestion" and use the package and then make your own informed changes, 
etc.  Once this sticky little problem is solved, that will be the case.  I 
can tell you from using the Package extensively now, I would find it quite 
cumbersome any other way. [The main reason is that icons are LOGICALLY 
grouped by function, but Windows does it strctly in alphanumeric order.]

2) Here is the big problem:

This package is 100% done in the total spirit and substance of freeware.  I 
have no monetary expectations whatsoever.  that said, I expect to have no 
NEGATIVE monetary problems as in the potential of a lawsuit from some 
greedy lawyer hired by freeware and shareware authors because I might 
include some of the binary of their packages, etc.

The consensus at this time is I have to provide the package [incomplete] 
and not have ANY code I didn't write in it.  Then a lot of documentation on 
what EXACTLY must be done by the END USER to wake up the features as they 
exist now.  this also means I have to provide proper cosmetics and messages 
for when you haven't included their offerings, so as to make it clear what 
and why, etc.

And of course, these sections then have to be added to the guide document 
as well.  [Obviously I have some notions of what will go in there, but that 
in and of itself is a lot of work].

3) The Guide document has to be functionally complete as in how to use all 
of the features; some require some manual editing of .BAT files due to the 
need to customize.  The package relies heavily on the availability of a 
.PDF file reader.  I'll have to document how you set which is the one you 
are reading.  Perhaps this can be accomplished by setting a global 
environment variable.  I already have other setup procedures that are 
literally just managers of one of those.  For example, the package must set 
an environment variable initially just to get installed.  This is called 
%PDP-8_DRIVE$ and is typically set to "C:" or "D:" or whatever.  On my 
system that is the basis for the Guide example figures, it is P: [The 
environment variable is just the two characters: uppper or lower case 
letter from C: through Z: followed by the : character; no more and no less  
That package is 100% done.

Similarly, there is a package to set an environment variable to your choice 
of three different compatible text editors: Windows notepad, notepade++ or 
EditPad.  You have to use at least one, and notepad is part of Windows 
itself.  The other two have to be separately installed and come in 32-bit 
and 64-bit versions, etc. and you have to install them in designated places 
on the %PDP-8_DRIVE% drive so all parts of the package can quick-change the 
choice merely by using the pertinent setup icon.  This is also completely 
done.

But perhaps there needs to be analogous support for a PDF package, I have 
been using Adobe Reader, but at least in theory, this should be possible to 
become another global variable.  I then have to try a candidate alternative 
and crank out all the hard-coded references to Adobe and change then to 
%PDF_Reader% or somesuch, etc.  An afternoon's work [when I get a free 
afternoon].

There may be a couple others that are similar.  I want it to be as flexible 
and configurable as possible.

The biggest job is actually the documentation which takes my time, quite a 
lot actually, and I get into mini-burnouts and then turn to doing something 
else productive, such as actually writing PDP-8 code with the Package as it 
is, or actually cranking out P?S/8 improvements.

The problem is that I am a "one man band" and have three different things 
going on, the dual problem of documentation of the other two each are 1/2 
of the third item beyond the obvious.

I have made some decent progress on nearing release of P?S/8 documents for 
specific aspects of P?S/8.  One of the goals is to spare everyone from the 
hard learning curve of OS/8, which all of us went through.  Yet, some 
children and random scientific researchers were able to use it usefully in 
about 10 minutes of introduction by me personally.  Perhaps this needs a 
Youtube video!

In any case, here are the list of important documents for which there is 
light at the end of the tunnel:

1) The P?S/8 keyboard monitor command guide.  If you don't know what to 
type and when and where to typie it, you can't use it.

2) The Patch Log file for P?S/8 Version 8Z, the latest complete release.  
This also points out various OS/8 analogous issues, limitations and 
work-arounds, and most importantly a jumbo sized Glossary of Terms since 
many will not be familiar with most of them. [A few are amusing add-ons 
which make fun reading, but must really are needed.]

3) The P?S/8 PAL reference guide.  this also includes a lot of valid 
programming techniques most will learn at least something from, and if you 
are a newbie, help you avoid a lot of programming pitfalls.

There are a few smaller-scale documents in various stages of being written, 
many are technical discussions of P?S/8 structures, especially directory 
structures.   When configuring P?S/8, you need to understand these to be 
capable of making the simple patches that might be needed to effect small 
changes, etc.

A valid criticism of P?S/8 is that, while with a little expertise these 
small patches are easy to make [some of them can even be automated with 
existing P?S/8 utilities] there are not a lot of P?S/8 utilities devoted 
only to making the changes mindless by a truly ignorant user.  Sorry, but 
this is always the lowest priority in any system that ever was or ever will 
be.  No pain no gain, and in this case, each is hardly any pain [but more 
than literally nothing.]

The configurations of P?S/8 supported in the SIMH environment [that is 
underlying to PEPS for Windows] is a "fantasy" version of TC01/TC08 
DECtape.  Real-world DECtapes come in the standard size of 2702 octal 
blocks of 129 words [where the 129th word is ignored, same as in OS/8 or 
any other system for the PDP-8 with the lone exception of the Disk Monitor 
System, which uses that word, and why the Disk Monitor System can only run 
on TC01/08 DECtame, DF32 and RF08 and nothing else whatsoever.  For those 
of us who bothered to look for the more-than-occasional DECtapes that are 
actually longer enough, there is a secondary standard of 3300 octal 
blocks.  There is both P?S/8 and OS/8 support for these tapes, and also 
some have recently been recovered and read on TD8E-based PDP-8/E systems.  
[The contents are important in the overall scheme of things and in some 
cases are the sole known source files of some important stuff].

But in the SIMh environment, ANY 12-bit length is possible, either TD8E or 
TC08 and you have to choose which length you want to use;   In OS/8, you 
have to patch PIP and then do the equivalent of the ZERO command [but in 
the CD mode].  In P?S/8, you have to patch [at most] three locations at the 
end of the TFS directory [actually only two typically].  The directory 
itself can hold no more than 84 files assuming the underlying storage is 
available; generally a good fit for a DECtape.

Each logical unit in P?S/8 can also have one extended-length file [no line 
numbers and no way to edit] that can be anywhere from the first block not 
covered by the TFS directory through the physical end of the device [or 
block 7777 which ever comes first].  By using the full "fantasy" mode, this 
essentially means that one file can be as large as about 2/3 of what can 
fit into an RK05 half-drive in OS/8 terms, etc.  Existing utilities can 
convert up to 17 files at a time into one extended-length file [and then 
that is completely reversible].  If you use existing extended files as part 
of the input, you can make an even llonger one on a different unit [if the 
space exists]  Most people haven't written anything long enoiugh to even 
get close to caring.

This requires a similar patch to another directory to effect a change.  [In 
the next release, this will be part of a new set of SET command sub-options 
as the structure itself is being rewirten; this is explained in the nearly 
complete patch log file.]

In any case, it is useful to use slightly different configurations to 
essentially have what in OS/8 terms would be termed "system" and 
"non-system" configurations.  In P?S/8, it is better described as 
"complete" and "demised" as all compatible logical units are generally able 
to do everything or anything, it's just a matter of how much since the 
space is finite, etc.  "Intermediate" users will understand what to do 
after reading some of what is available, etc.

For those with actual compatible machines, at this point you have to 
contact me; physical releases have been done for TC01/08 and recently RX01 
family.  Some others are quite close to being tried out on SIMH such as 
RK8E/RK05 and the TD8E [These are all supported in SIMH in fairly good 
ways].  Due to some unknown limitation of SIMH, the TC01/TC08 release 
requires a one-word patch, fully documented in the eco patch log file.

Certain people have programs to transfer SIMH-conmpatible images that can 
be emailed to the actual hardware.  some of the methods are OS/8-centric 
but can be part of the solution in conjuncrion with my Kermit-12 utilities 
for OS/8.  As long as the underlying media is compatible between P/S/8 and 
OS/8, this will work fine [this is the situation in about 90 percent of all 
possible configurations.]


On Thursday, September 27, 2018 at 8:01:28 AM UTC-4, cjl wrote:
>
> As I do more work for the next release, I will note some of the changes as 
> they come about.
>
> Sometimes the unexpected changes cause minor snags.  Here is a tiny one I 
> just worked out a fix for.  [Note:  I could retrofit these two in Version 
> 8Z, but they are extremely minor in terms of consequence to systems prior 
> to 9A]
>
> Some of you are familiar with the unique P?S/8 feature of binary loading 
> invented by Richard Lary caled [by him, I'm not changing it!] "Slurp" to 
> describe how it works.
>
> The first incarnation was done in the R-L Monitor System [and was the ONLY 
> choice back then].  The idea is this:
>
> You can load binary files without a handler in memory and without a net 
> buffer [other than inside of the loader itself].  The contents of 
> 07600-07777 are thus changed over to the following:
>
> 1) A reboot of at least this particular hardware-dependent version of the 
> operating system.  In this case, the bootstrap used works with R-L, P?S/8, 
> Dibol-9, POLY BASIC, EDU-System 30, COS 300/310, OS/8, the DECtape Library 
> System etc.  Just about all EXCEPT the Disk Monitor System [because it is 
> incompatible on the key issue, where to be waiting for DMA overlay.  Just 
> failure to actually know what to do;  The Library System started the 
> convention, and it is fine and clearly works for all of those systems, and 
> since it came first, there is no excuse, yet the DMS being incompatible 
> causes the more general bootstrap to include it to be more complicated 
> BECAUSE it is incompatible and has to be read in non-overlayed and then 
> started up, etc.
>
> This of course starts at 07600 so that programs can exit universally.  
> This bootstrap code then reboots R-L.
>
> P?S/8 updates this on the TC01/08 with a frill feature.  You can boot 
> P/S/8 for the TC01/08 on ANY logical unit.  The extended bootstrap follows 
> the general scheme of other devices.  The short versions are based on 
> zeroed out bits in the AC, the extended versions have drive-specific bits 
> in the AC that are used to continue the bootup process,etc.
>
> In the case of the DECtape hardware, the logical unit is places in 
> bits[0-2] because the hardware wants them there, etc.
>
> Thus, in P?S/8, the boot is particularized to the drive the original 
> bootup was perfornmed to, and that dooesn't change ever [unless you want to 
> run another boot program].
>
> Some of the code in the bootup is also shared with the Slurp loader, the 
> main occupant of the rest of the page.
>
> For definition purposes, the bootstrap is defined as at most 07600-07641 
> [formerly up to 07642], but shortened by one to add am improvement that is 
> paper-tape oriented, the subject of another post].
>
> That means that if you move these words into locations 07600-07641, you 
> can do a reboot by starting at 07600. [Note: The purpose is NOT to be the 
> shortest, but rather to be a reasonable length and start at 07600.  In the 
> case, part of the design is compromised for the benefit of the slurp 
> loader, which uses portions of the boot code as subroutines and even 
> contrived constants, etc.
>
> The slurp loader complete definition includes the subset that is the 
> reboot code; it can use any of the reboot code words as necessary, etc.  
> [Actually, a stand-alone version of the reboot code can be quite a bit 
> shorter, but then a needed word for slurp loader purposes would be gone 
> because the optimization depends on it being where it is.  Actually, the 
> highest word is in 07640, thus there is actually one word to spare in the 
> new definition, and to my knowledge this is the longest such code in all 
> the supported systems].
>
> The TC08 slurp loader has its own starting address further up the page.  
> It does the usual block searching with one exception: it forces an initial 
> search FORWARD because this is generally the case given the layout of the 
> system.
>
> The P?S/8 version supports extended-memory loading while R-L is strictly 
> 4K [but the extension for extended memory was always defined.]  once the 
> starting block of the latest file to load is located, the read data mode is 
> selected.  So far, this sounds like the system device handler [but what 
> follows is totally different].
>
> The current address is set to point to a temporary inside of the slurp 
> loader area, and the word count is zeroed.  DMA will start in a few 
> microseconds by the nature of DECtape DMA transfers.
>
> The slurp loader continuously reads the word count checking if it has 
> changed from 0 -> 1.  That means that in a couple more cycles, the latest 
> word is now transferred into the word the Current Address pointed to.
>
> This latest word is then processed.  Then the whole process repeats.  The 
> Word Count is again zeroes, and the current address is backed up again to 
> point to the same location, etc.  Wait for it to go from 0  to one, etc.
>
> This happens nominally every 133 microseconds.  The four operations are as 
> follows:
>
> If the latest data word is actually 12-bits intended to be loaded, store 
> it into the intended memory location and bump up the current loading 
> location counter.
>
> If the latest data is an origin setting, load that into the current 
> loading location counter.
>
> If the word is a field setting, it is in the form of CDF x0 where x is the 
> field setting.  Stick that in-line in an instruction just before the DCA I 
> COUNTER instruction so that all data is loaded as intended starting with 
> any data words that follow, etc.
>
> If the word is an end-of-file indicator, go on to the next file, and 
> repeat everything; process all passed files until the very last one in a 
> passed list of input files [located in 07757-07777 or less].  After that, 
> JMP to a startup routine which consists of a CIF CDF xx instruction 
> followed by a JMP I 7756.
>
> While only P?S/8 has the extended memory stuff, R-L started all of this.  
> 07756 is the location where the =xxxx parameter is stored from the original 
> RUn command thus where the program starts up.  If the =xxxx is not given, 
> it is the address of a safe halting piece of code; the user has to work the 
> front panel to do anything else [but this is by design. If you were using 
> the BIN loader, you are in the same position.]
>
> P?S/8 adds a minor feature.  You cannot pass =7777 to any program, but 
> that is passed by default.  thus, now programs can do whatever they wish if 
> the value is 7777,  in this case, the default is the address of that CIF 
> CDF instruction.  07756 points to a word just before which halts.  [It is 
> actually a compound OPR instruction that cannot skip and does halt, 7772, a 
> needed constant elsewhere.  Pressing CONTINUE just then executes the same 
> words so it is a safe repeating situation, etc  That is unless the user 
> passes explicit starting information to the BIN utility program that places 
> this code, etc.
>
> The default field is 0, but you can set /1 through /7 to specify extended 
> memory start along with the =xxxx to any starting address xxxx, etc.
>
> A minor detail of the next system BIN program:  It will check to make sure 
> that at most only one of /1=/7 is set and disallow it if the =xxxx is not 
> passed. [Upgrading releases is a matter of things tiny, small and in some 
> cases large.]
>
> But P?S/8 adds another thing over all of the above.  [note:  This is 
> counter-intuitive, but correct, so send your intuition to where the sun 
> don't shine if need be].
>
> Assuming all of the above happened already, what if there was another file 
> passed at the end of the list that is "friendly malware" that perverts the 
> slurp loader in "useful ways"?  Well, there is, and this is what it does:
>
> Block 0 is the image of the system device handler, but it is not necessary 
> to load any location past 7745.  [7747 is a one-word empty, and the rest 
> are temporaries or the data-break stuff, etc this is inside of the system 
> device handler, etc.[.]
>
> Thus, selective overlay of the slurp loader makes it turn into a reload 
> MOST of bock 0 into 07600-07745  Now you have the system device handler 
> capability restored, yet you have not written on the tape for any loading 
> operation!  [This is in part why the slurp loader is so fast.  The TC01/08 
> slurp loader is FASTER than OS/8 using ABSLDR on RK8E/RK05. [Confirmed with 
> a stop-watch to the horrified eyes of DEC PDP-8 programming group members 
> who fell into cognitive dissonance; they literally couldn't believe a 
> DECtape could be faster than an RK05!].
>
> Normallly up to seventeen files can be passed to the Slurp loader, but 
> this magic "file" has to be passed last, thus the effective upper limit is 
> "only": 16 files.  Note: Slurp format approaches 6/7 of the efficiency of 
> core-image yet is not block-image oiriented, it allows random access 
> loading without restriction.  thus, even sixteen files can load nearly 24K 
> of memory in one command, and of course rarely is that much done in one 
> command. [and you could do more with a another command, but these are 
> contrivances for special circumstances assuming running on 32K. It's not a 
> great "sacrific" to have to split the equivalent of 18-19 files into two 
> groups.  Also, Version 9A should have the BINCON utility that can combine 
> up to 17 files into one extended-length file.  Then all of 32K can be 
> loaded in 3 files or less].  Typical usage is one or two files, and three 
> is more than enough to fill all of field 0, etc.]
>
> this last "file" is an image on the O/S in a reserved block.  Each 
> configuration has its own.  The recently re-released RX01 P?S/8 has its own 
> analogous one stored in the same corresponding logical block [but the 
> contents does the same to the RX slurp loader, etc.]
>
> The command to accomplish this includes the /I command-line option switch
>
> Thus, you get the seemingly impossible:  You started with the system 
> device handler in 07600, and you loaded memory, and in the end you still 
> have the system device handler.  [Dark grey magic revealed!]
>
> Now a design comparison with OS/8:
>
> OS/8 exits to 07600 as well, but it ALWAYS starts with writing out part of 
> memory and then swaps in a (compromised) keyboard monitor that can only 
> accomplish very little, so feeble that CCL had to be added on [and is NOT 
> RECOMMENDED FOR USE on tape-based systems, official DEC published in 
> manuals!].
>
> This is ALWAYS done, yet rarely do you take advantage of WHY it was 
> written out [for the SAVE command, hardly ever actually used compared to 
> ALWAYS writing on every exit by every program to 07600.  Also, 07605 is an 
> OS/89 ism, not the standard which is ALWAYS 07600.
>
> P?S/8 has another thing in the equation I haven't mentioned:  Unlike OS.8, 
> you do not need extended memory to run anything.  This is because OS/8 was 
> not able to get an embedded call to the system device handler into field 0 
> alongside of the handler, so they kludged it into field 1, thus creating 
> the cheap excuse of also reserving field 1 7600-7777 [and in turn forcing 
> the need for extended memory even though perhaps your only usage is to load 
> programs that completely fit in 4K\
>
> In P?S/8, there is an embedded call to the system handler entered at 
> 07632.  When the operation above is completed, the contents of these 
> locations are those that are on the tape by default.
>
> What is actually there is a call to write out ALL of field 0 and THEN JMP 
> 7600 for the usual non-writing bootstrap that is the usual case.
>
> As such, if you use the BIN loader and specify /I =7632, your program is 
> saved [that requires writing of course] and then the rest is the normal 
> bootup read-only.
>
> As a user convenience, there is an alias system program entry for BIN 
> called GET.  It forces /1-/7 off, forces /I on, and forces =7632 and 
> otherwise is the BIN utility to pass specified binary input files to be 
> loaded by the slurp loader.
>
> Thus, in P?S/8 if you WANT to write, you use the GET command.
>
> After that, your program is in the virtual scratch area for all of field 0 
> which is in blocks 0020-0057 [aka sometimes as the % and $ files, 
> respectively for each half of that space; all files are 2K long].
>
> You can use P?S/8 ODT on the program, and the virtual image is always 
> updated.  There is a BSAVE facility to save programs back into slurp 
> files.  The arguments resemble the OS/8 SAVE command arguments, the 
> difference is these are not groupedout, but are actual save definitions,, 
> even down to one single word if you wish.
>
> [Note: In general, extended memory is ignored by P?S/8.  However, system 
> programs such as P?S/8 PAL use as much extended memory as they can access, 
> thus, if any extended memory areas were changes and need saving, use the 
> appropriate command elements to the BSAVE command which is 32K oriented.  
> ODT is a proper superset of OS/8 ODT, etc.
>
> Thus, you write on the system device ONLY when necessary, NOT mindlessly 
> every single time. [And on DECtape that means running write-locked if you 
> wish; can't screw up and destroy the system tape that way!].  Also, errors 
> in the system handler, such as write-lock error, can be retried by pressing 
> CONTINUE.
>
> Those familiar with the PDP-9 ADSS/9 system may be aware of IOPS4! this is 
> a built-in kernel function to allow a retry.  [And later, after P?S/8 
> copied it, MS-DOS did.]  In P?S/8, there is the OPTIONAL Logical Console 
> Overlay.  When enabled, it is loaded into the HIGHEST field of memory [and 
> not field 1 unless it's an 8K machine].  All system device errors are 
> intercepted and you get all the call particulars when it failed to 
> complete, etc.  You can retry or abort to 07600.  OS/8 totally lacks this 
> conceptually, yet requires 8K. P?S/8 requires 8K or more only if you enable 
> the Overlay, etc.
>
>
> Now, back to the growing pain for the next release:
>
> Without going into the gyrations of how the logical unit used to boot is 
> preserved unconditionally, the problem boils down to this:
>
> After the image of (most of) the contents of block 0 are reloaded [into 
> most of 07600-07777] there is a problem:  The default contents of the 
> embedded system device handler call are lacking the proper current 
> definition of the bootup unit. [this is NOT written to the block ever; it 
> just has to be dealt with at the moment what it happens to be].
>
> In P?S/8, there are some reserved words in the kernel, namely:
>
> 07604-6 - All 36 command-line switches are stored here when a system 
> program is loaded.  [Same notions as OS/8, just specifically different 
> locations, etc.
>
> 07607 - This is the output file count.
>
> In P/S/8, all files are pre-allocated and all are 2K long.  The 
> execution-class commands are prepared to pre-create them if necessary, 
> etc.  By the time the program executes, this is what is passed:
>
> A list starts in 07757 ending in as much as 07777.  the last one is 
> followed by 0000 as a sentinel [unless the list is full max, in which case 
> it self-terminates because of clever use of the ISZ instruction].
>
> In R-L, they are all input files.  [PAL II only wrote to IMPLIED areas of 
> the system tape, and then after the fact and only if possible, a command 
> was used to save that to a named file in the directory.  And if not 
> possible, you always had to assemble the relevant program to run it, etc.
>
> In P?S.8, there might be output files.  All output files [if any] are 
> passed before all input files [if any].  the contents of 07607 is the 
> output file count (which could be anywhere from 0 through 17 depending]/
>
> The key point is that all four of these locations are only meaingful 
> during normal program execution, thus are merely placeholders on the tape 
> image of block 0, etc.
>
> But because of this last problem, these locations are loaded with the 
> following:
>
> TAD 7634 /GET THE CURRENT WRITE-OUT FUNCTION
> TAD SBTFUN  /ADD ON THE ACTUAL UNIT BITS THAT APPLY
> DCA 7634 /UPDATE THE EMBEDDED CALL
> JMP elsewhere.
>
> The jump in this case is to the DIF CDF instruction that starts the 
> startup process of the slurp loader /I operation. [because that is why 
> these are here!].
>
> The new problem is this:
>
> As of Version 9A, the P?S/8 DATE is being upgraded.
>
> The present [somewhat embarrasing, read kludge here] situation [that 
> applies to version 8Z[ is as follows:
>
> Programs that require the full date carry the high-order bits 
> INTRINSICALLY within the program's images in a format any way they want to.
>
> This is the present formula:
>
> Each such program stores a local version of the base year group as a 
> constant.
>
> For example, 0 means that the date starts in 1960, 1=1968, 2-1976, etc.
>
> The present P?S/8 Version 8Z is patched so all of them start in 2016.
>
> The rest of the date is stored to the nearest day in 07610.  It uses a 
> quirky method that can store from say 2016 through 2026 [and a little more, 
> but the P?S/8 DATE command rounds it back to the highest complete year 
> possible].
>
> Thus, you have to patch this every eight years nearly indefinitely., etc.
>
> The value in 07607 is being upgraded.  In version 9A, it will instead be 
> this:
>
> bits[7-11] still mean the output file count.  But you have to do an AND 
> (37) or equivalent in all programs that work with this [a VERY minor issue].
>
> Bit[0] is a parameter for the forthcoming P?S/8 SHELL optional overlay.  
> This is one of its fundamental parameters:
>
> P?S/8 SHELL needs a far larger area to communicate file and related stuff 
> than OS/8.  There are many reasons such as:
>
> 1) The program name will be stored there. As is practiced in other 
> systems, a program can change what it does by examining the name it was 
> created as, and actually change what it does accordingly.
>
> 2) OS/8 names are 6.2, P?S/8 names are 12.4.
>
> 3) OS/8 names are upper-case only, P?S/8 names are case-RETENTIVE [like 
> OS/2, not case sensitive, when a file is created, the case of each 
> character is retained, but searches are caseless, etc.]
>
> 4) File lists will be shorter than P?S/8, typically two or three at most.  
> There is the notion of the positional default for the first and second and 
> third file.  All of this has to be stored somewhere, etc.
>
> 5) 128K startup information [for KT8A].
>
> 6) Operational info for the User File Operator (UFO) which is essentially 
> what the OS/8 USR never was - on steroids.  Also, it will all be page and 
> field relocatable, so no silly "rules" about exactly where things must be, 
> etc.
>
> 7) Ample room for future expansion [no corners painted into like OS/8 was 
> doomed by 1977]
>
> 8) Much larger directory capability on far larger devices.  [The entire 
> RK8E in 4 logical units, not 8 "half-drives" etc.]
>
> That's really more than enough reasons, etc.
>
> Bit[0] if 1 means That the information area [dubbed FCA for File 
> Communications Area] is located in field x 5400-5777. x is the physical 
> highest field of memory.  The reasons for this are:
>
> 1) Field x 6000-7777 are reserved for system devices that cannot be 
> supported in 4K.  Unlike OS/8, this is a lot more than just giving 124 more 
> words i 27600-27773, with that much more memory, elegant device handling 
> including error recovery is possible.  The DECmate RX50 handler may 
> actually be trouble fitting into "only" 8 more pages!
>
> 2) Field x 0000-5377 is where the optional Logical Console Overlay is 
> loaded.
>
> Thus, this is the sensible two-page menmory area IF it is actually in 
> memory!
>
> If Bit[0] is set to 0, it means that the FCA resides in two low system 
> blocks TBD, but something like say 9972-0073..  That puts them near the UFO 
> storage blocks.  As such, the perceived degradation is far less than 
> actual.  [And it will be tested to make sure] but if you don't have the 
> memory, you do what you must.  Of course if you have 32K,, this hardly ever 
> happens :-).  But on 4K machines, it is mandatory, and perhaps recommended 
> for some 8K or so system configurations, etc.  If you can, try it both 
> ways, or even switch periodically, etc.
>
> The date is being totally revamped:
>
> Location 07610 will be reformatted as the low-order 12 bits of the date 
> [much like OS/8, except the bits are weighted in a useful order instead of 
> an arbitrary one.  The older the date, the older the file, etc.]/
>
> Bits [1-6] of 07607 are the full date extension of 8-year base groups, 
> unconditionally.
>
> You will be able to play with the date for ANY day in the range of 
> 01-Jan-1900 through 31-Dec-2311.
>
> [Note: This means that the every 4 century rule has to be added.  the 
> present scheme is wrong after 2099.]
>
> 01-Jan-1900 is a Monday.  Keep going except for leap years except that 
> each leap century there isn't a leap year except that there is anyway every 
> 400 years.
>
>
> As such,, the tape has to retain bits[0-6] permanently.  This word is no 
> longer available for that /I last-instant fixup code described above.  
> [Well, it is one word shorter now.]
>
> I was able to bum out as many as three words from the overall situation 
> involving Slurp loader, /I code and the actual TC08 system device handler.  
> As such, I will no longer need to store permanently into any of them.
>
> Note: The CORE command sets some values in 07611 currently.  Some cleanup 
> is needed in the command to not accidentally store permanently that which 
> could change if you bring the tape to another machine.  Fortunately, the 
> CORE ALL command always resets everything, but in 9A it will be slightly 
> cleaner, etc.
>
> All of this explanation over the need to not use 7 bits as practiced on 
> the TC01/TC08.  [Lots if little battles before it can be released as the 
> next whole version].
>
> cjl
>
>
>
>
