><br>3) The P?S/8 PAL reference guide.&nbsp; this also includes a lot of va=
lid programming techniques most will learn at least something from, and if =
you are a newbie, help you avoid a lot of programming pitfalls.<br><br>Ther=
e are a few smaller-scale documents in various stages of being written, man=
y are technical discussions of P?S/8 structures, especially directory struc=
tures.&nbsp; &nbsp;When configuring P?S/8, you need to understand these to =
be capable of making the simple patches that might be needed to effect smal=
l changes, etc.<br><br>A valid criticism of P?S/8 is that, while with a lit=
tle expertise these small patches are easy to make [some of them can even b=
e automated with existing P?S/8 utilities] there are not a lot of P?S/8 uti=
lities devoted only to making the changes mindless by a truly ignorant user=
.&nbsp; Sorry, but this is always the lowest priority in any system that ev=
er was or ever will be.&nbsp; No pain no gain, and in this case, each is ha=
rdly any pain [but more than literally nothing.]<br><br>The configurations =
of P?S/8 supported in the SIMH environment [that is underlying to PEPS for =
Windows] is a "fantasy" version of TC01/TC08 DECtape.&nbsp; Real-world DECt=
apes come in the standard size of 2702 octal blocks of 129 words [where the=
 129th word is ignored, same as in OS/8 or any other system for the PDP-8 w=
ith the lone exception of the Disk Monitor System, which uses that word, an=
d why the Disk Monitor System can only run on TC01/08 DECtame, DF32 and RF0=
8 and nothing else whatsoever.&nbsp; For those of us who bothered to look f=
or the more-than-occasional DECtapes that are actually longer enough, there=
 is a secondary standard of 3300 octal blocks.&nbsp; There is both P?S/8 an=
d OS/8 support for these tapes, and also some have recently been recovered =
and read on TD8E-based PDP-8/E systems.&nbsp; [The contents are important i=
n the overall scheme of things and in some cases are the sole known source =
