bit length is possible, either TD8E or TC08 and you have to choose which le=
ngth you want to use;&nbsp; &nbsp;In OS/8, you have to patch PIP and then d=
o the equivalent of the ZERO command [but in the CD mode].&nbsp; In P?S/8, =
you have to patch [at most] three locations at the end of the TFS directory=
 [actually only two typically].&nbsp; The directory itself can hold no more=
 than 84 files assuming the underlying storage is available; generally a go=
od fit for a DECtape.<br><br>Each logical unit in P?S/8 can also have one e=
xtended-length file [no line numbers and no way to edit] that can be anywhe=
re from the first block not covered by the TFS directory through the physic=
al end of the device [or block 7777 which ever comes first].&nbsp; By using=
 the full "fantasy" mode, this essentially means that one file can be as la=
rge as about 2/3 of what can fit into an RK05 half-drive in OS/8 terms, etc=
.&nbsp; Existing utilities can convert up to 17 files at a time into one ex=
tended-length file [and then that is completely reversible].&nbsp; If you u=
se existing extended files as part of the input, you can make an even llong=
er one on a different unit [if the space exists]&nbsp; Most people haven't =
written anything long enoiugh to even get close to caring.<br><br>This requ=
ires a similar patch to another directory to effect a change.&nbsp; [In the=
 next release, this will be part of a new set of SET command sub-options as=
 the structure itself is being rewirten; this is explained in the nearly co=
mplete patch log file.]</div><div><br></div><div>In any case, it is useful =
to use slightly different configurations to essentially have what in OS/8 t=
erms would be termed "system" and "non-system" configurations.&nbsp; In P?S=
/8, it is better described as "complete" and "demised" as all compatible lo=
gical units are generally able to do everything or anything, it's just a ma=
tter of how much since the space is finite, etc.&nbsp; "Intermediate" users=
