es for OS/8.&nbsp; As long as the underlying media is compatible between P/=
S/8 and OS/8, this will work fine [this is the situation in about 90 percen=
t of all possible configurations.]</div></div></html-blob><html-blob><div d=
ir=3D"ltr"><div><br><br>On Thursday, September 27, 2018 at 8:01:28 AM UTC-4=
, cjl wrote:<blockquote class=3D"gmail_quote" style=3D"margin:0;margin-left=
:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex"><div dir=3D"ltr">As I d=
o more work for the next release, I will note some of the changes as they c=
ome about.<br><br>Sometimes the unexpected changes cause minor snags.&nbsp;=
 Here is a tiny one I just worked out a fix for.&nbsp; [Note:&nbsp; I could=
 retrofit these two in Version 8Z, but they are extremely minor in terms of=
 consequence to systems prior to 9A]<br><br>Some of you are familiar with t=
he unique P?S/8 feature of binary loading invented by Richard Lary caled [b=
y him, I'm not changing it!] "Slurp" to describe how it works.<br><br>The f=
irst incarnation was done in the R-L Monitor System [and was the ONLY choic=
e back then].&nbsp; The idea is this:<br><br>You can load binary files with=
out a handler in memory and without a net buffer [other than inside of the =
loader itself].&nbsp; The contents of 07600-07777 are thus changed over to =
the following:<br><br>1) A reboot of at least this particular hardware-depe=
ndent version of the operating system.&nbsp; In this case, the bootstrap us=
ed works with R-L, P?S/8, Dibol-9, POLY BASIC, EDU-System 30, COS 300/310, =
OS/8, the DECtape Library System etc.&nbsp; Just about all EXCEPT the Disk =
Monitor System [because it is incompatible on the key issue, where to be wa=
iting for DMA overlay.&nbsp; Just failure to actually know what to do;&nbsp=
; The Library System started the convention, and it is fine and clearly wor=
ks for all of those systems, and since it came first, there is no excuse, y=
et the DMS being incompatible causes the more general bootstrap to include =
it to be more complicated BECAUSE it is incompatible and has to be read in =
non-overlayed and then started up, etc.<br><br>This of course starts at 076=
00 so that programs can exit universally.&nbsp; This bootstrap code then re=
boots R-L.<br><br>P?S/8 updates this on the TC01/08 with a frill feature.&n=
bsp; You can boot P/S/8 for the TC01/08 on ANY logical unit.&nbsp; The exte=
nded bootstrap follows the general scheme of other devices.&nbsp; The short=
 versions are based on zeroed out bits in the AC, the extended versions hav=
e drive-specific bits in the AC that are used to continue the bootup proces=
s,etc.<br><br>In the case of the DECtape hardware, the logical unit is plac=
es in bits[0-2] because the hardware wants them there, etc.<br><br>Thus, in=
 P?S/8, the boot is particularized to the drive the original bootup was per=
fornmed to, and that dooesn't change ever [unless you want to run another b=
oot program].<br><br>Some of the code in the bootup is also shared with the=
 Slurp loader, the main occupant of the rest of the page.<br><br>For defini=
tion purposes, the bootstrap is defined as at most 07600-07641 [formerly up=
 to 07642], but shortened by one to add am improvement that is paper-tape o=
riented, the subject of another post].<br><br>That means that if you move t=
hese words into locations 07600-07641, you can do a reboot by starting at 0=
7600. [Note: The purpose is NOT to be the shortest, but rather to be a reas=
onable length and start at 07600.&nbsp; In the case, part of the design is =
compromised for the benefit of the slurp loader, which uses portions of the=
 boot code as subroutines and even contrived constants, etc.<br><br>The slu=
rp loader complete definition includes the subset that is the reboot code; =
it can use any of the reboot code words as necessary, etc.&nbsp; [Actually,=
 a stand-alone version of the reboot code can be quite a bit shorter, but t=
hen a needed word for slurp loader purposes would be gone because the optim=
ization depends on it being where it is.&nbsp; Actually, the highest word i=
s in 07640, thus there is actually one word to spare in the new definition,=
 and to my knowledge this is the longest such code in all the supported sys=
tems].<br><br>The TC08 slurp loader has its own starting address further up=
 the page.&nbsp; It does the usual block searching with one exception: it f=
orces an initial search FORWARD because this is generally the case given th=
e layout of the system.<br><br>The P?S/8 version supports extended-memory l=
oading while R-L is strictly 4K [but the extension for extended memory was =
always defined.]&nbsp; once the starting block of the latest file to load i=
s located, the read data mode is selected.&nbsp; So far, this sounds like t=
he system device handler [but what follows is totally different].<br><br>Th=
e current address is set to point to a temporary inside of the slurp loader=
 area, and the word count is zeroed.&nbsp; DMA will start in a few microsec=
onds by the nature of DECtape DMA transfers.<br><br>The slurp loader contin=
uously reads the word count checking if it has changed from 0 -&gt; 1.&nbsp=
; That means that in a couple more cycles, the latest word is now transferr=
ed into the word the Current Address pointed to.<br><br>This latest word is=
 then processed.&nbsp; Then the whole process repeats.&nbsp; The Word Count=
 is again zeroes, and the current address is backed up again to point to th=
e same location, etc.&nbsp; Wait for it to go from 0&nbsp; to one, etc.<br>=
<br>This happens nominally every 133 microseconds.&nbsp; The four operation=
s are as follows:<br><br>If the latest data word is actually 12-bits intend=
ed to be loaded, store it into the intended memory location and bump up the=
 current loading location counter.<br><br>If the latest data is an origin s=
etting, load that into the current loading location counter.<br><br>If the =
word is a field setting, it is in the form of CDF x0 where x is the field s=
etting.&nbsp; Stick that in-line in an instruction just before the DCA I CO=
UNTER instruction so that all data is loaded as intended starting with any =
data words that follow, etc.<br><br>If the word is an end-of-file indicator=
, go on to the next file, and repeat everything; process all passed files u=
ntil the very last one in a passed list of input files [located in 07757-07=
777 or less].&nbsp; After that, JMP to a startup routine which consists of =
a CIF CDF xx instruction followed by a JMP I 7756.<br><br>While only P?S/8 =
has the extended memory stuff, R-L started all of this.&nbsp; 07756 is the =
location where the =3Dxxxx parameter is stored from the original RUn comman=
d thus where the program starts up.&nbsp; If the =3Dxxxx is not given, it i=
s the address of a safe halting piece of code; the user has to work the fro=
nt panel to do anything else [but this is by design. If you were using the =
BIN loader, you are in the same position.]<br><br>P?S/8 adds a minor featur=
e.&nbsp; You cannot pass =3D7777 to any program, but that is passed by defa=
ult.&nbsp; thus, now programs can do whatever they wish if the value is 777=
7,&nbsp; in this case, the default is the address of that CIF CDF instructi=
